Modifications made for this study
---------------------------------
Both changes were required to evaluate Shaker on Python projects and are
contributions of the accompanying paper. Neither alters Shaker's detection
mechanism or its stress configurations.

1. Single-test execution (`-stp` / `--specific-tests-path`)
   Upstream Shaker accepts only a project directory and stresses the whole
   test suite. The ground truth used in this study identifies flakiness per
   individual test, so `shaker.py` and `tool_pytest.py` were extended to accept
   a single Pytest node id and run only that test.

2. Heterogeneous dependency resolution
   Upstream Shaker assumes a single dependency-declaration format. The projects
   sampled here declare dependencies in several formats, so dependency
   resolution was generalised to install each project before running its test.

Removals
--------
`analytics.py` was removed from this copy. It was an optional upstream
reporting script that POSTed run summaries to a third-party endpoint that is no
longer operated. It was never imported or invoked by Shaker itself, so removing
it does not change any behaviour; it is dropped here so that nothing in this
artifact contacts an external service beyond cloning the subject projects and
installing their dependencies. The upstream copy is unaffected.

The stress configurations in `shaker/stressConfigurations.json` are inherited
from the original study UNCHANGED; leaving them untouched is what the
accompanying paper sets out to evaluate.

Third-party components invoked at runtime
-----------------------------------------
  stress-ng (GPL-2.0) is executed as a SEPARATE PROCESS via subprocess, not
  linked into Shaker, and is not redistributed with this artifact. Its licence
  therefore does not extend to this software.
